  "worked_solution": "We have a horizontal tangent when\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal tangents, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${x}^{′}(\\theta ).$ \n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the values of $\\theta $ where ${y}^{′}(\\theta )=0.$\n\nBy writing down and solving the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. However, among the solutions above, only one belongs to the required domain, namely\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Check to see whether our candidate has ${x}^{′}(\\theta )=0.$ \n\nWe substitute our candidate solution into ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and check that ${x}^{′}(\\theta )\\ne 0.$ If ${x}^{′}(\\theta )=0,$ further investigation is necessary.\n\n- Substituting ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-8(1) \\\\  & =-8 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, our curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227550/assets/01c5231ee08bc44cb666d6e3256b6769c59e849ad8a8b040f751592fdd6932c8.png)",
